      "text": "AG1\n\nNew chat\n\nProjects\nproject\nOpen project\n\nUse webSearch to find official Rust documentation for std::time::Instant and std::time::Du\nChats\nArchived chats\nGitHub\nMCP\nSettings\nUse webSearch to find official Rust documentation for std::time::Instant and std::time::Du\nproject\n\n\n\n\nUse webSearch to find official Rust documentation for std::time::Instant and std::time::Duration, then use webFetch to read one result. Give a brief progress sentence before searching and before fetching. Explain their difference in three concise bullets, linking your sources. Do not create or edit any files.\n\nWorked for 1m 12s\n6 tools\nThoughts\nProgress: Both Instant and Duration documentation pages fetched successfully. Here is the comparison:\n\nstd::time::Instant vs std::time::Duration\nInstant is an opaque point-in-time measurement from a monotonically nondecreasing clock. It represents \"when\" something happened and is useful for measuring elapsed time (e.g., benchmarking, timeouts). It cannot be converted to a wall-clock timestamp and is only meaningful relative to other instants from the same clock. Source\nDuration is a span of time — a quantity, not a point. It represents \"how long\" something took, composed of whole seconds plus subsecond (nanosecond) precision. It is used to express intervals, such as system timeouts or elapsed-time results from Instant::elapsed(). Source\nRelationship: An Instant measures when; a Duration measures how long. Subtracting one Instant from another yields a Duration (e.g., instant.elapsed()), making Duration the bridge between two points on the monotonic clock.\n\nTokens · 1621 in · 311 out · 1891 cached\n\n\n\nAsk before changes\n\nQwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_XL\n\nEnter to send · Shift Enter for a new line",
